WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.539
Module 14 is the web build — the same app, running in an ordinary browser tab, no install.

2
00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode is the architectural trick that makes that possible without forking the UI.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.960
One command: npm run web:dev, then open localhost 5175.

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.000
That's the entire app, running with no Tauri process behind it at all.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.770
The desktop app never talks to Rust directly — it goes through five Tauri imports: core, plugin-fs, plugin-http, api event, plugin-opener, and plugin-updater.

6
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:28.787
The web build swaps each of those five imports for a browser implementation living in web slash shims.

7
00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:31.000
Same src tree, same features, zero forked UI.

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Walking the shim table: core dot ts is the command dispatcher, fs dot ts is backed by the browser's Origin Private File System, http dot ts uses the native fetch so real CORS rules apply, event dot ts is a local no-op bus, and secret dot ts is the browser's own secret store.

9
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:53.738
What still can't run in a tab: local models over localhost, since an https page can't reach plain http localhost — that's mixed content; stdio MCP servers; the device mesh; and the native in-app browser.

10
00:00:53.738 --> 00:00:57.000
Each just fails as an ordinary “not available” error, never a crash.

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.302
One update since the curriculum was written: local models are no longer purely a desktop feature.

12
00:01:01.302 --> 00:01:08.000
On a GPU that supports WebGPU, the browser build can now run models in-tab too — that's next episode's territory, alongside browser speech recognition.

13
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.096
The seam is in place.

14
00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:14.000
Next: a full spoken conversation, transcription and speech, entirely in that same browser tab.

